# Printed-page overlays and local printing

## Scope and existing evidence

Support printed-page flags and background-free overlays in the Rust compositor
with explicit printer intent, leaving physical plotting out of scope.

The historical accepted theoretical design is preserved in completed P1–P9
records. This record is **open executable integration work**. It must not be
closed merely because a prior design was accepted or a different
implementation was proposed.

## Colored ink, date and preprinted two-page spool parity — 2026-11-04

The independent fake-CUPS print test now submits exact native Rust bytes
from a two-page preprinted PDF with a validated 2026-11-04 date, page
numbers, rounded border and one colored diagonal pen segment on page two.
It verifies that suppressing existing ruling reduces paper-mark strokes
while retaining those printed annotations and page-specific ink, and that
`--dry-run` cannot contact the fake spool. Explicit submission reproduces
the PDF bytes unchanged through the fixed CUPS argument contract.

All three print tests pass, and the full notebook-paper crate now passes
strict all-target Clippy, including the previously failing test fixture.
No real local print queue was contacted; physical alignment, saved image
layers and complete notebook acceptance remain open.

## Review-locked local print bytes (partial) — 2026-11-04

The opt-in `atrament-print --expect-sha256` now compares an exact 64-digit
reviewed digest against the same bounded PDF snapshot sent to CUPS. `--dry-run`
reports SHA-256 without spawning a printer. The native FIPS SHA-256 primitive
is reused; the print adapter never reopens the pathname after checksum
validation. Fake-spool acceptance covers unchanged native two-page PDF bytes,
refusal of malformed/duplicate hashes, and refusal when a different but still
valid PDF replaces the reviewed file; no print occurs on checksum mismatch.

This is an explicit safety guard for a previously previewed PDF, not a PDF
security parser, proof of printer output, or complete saved image workflow.

## Exact PDF version-line refusal before local spool (partial) — 2026-11-04

The explicit `atrament-print` selected-file gate now requires a complete
header line with PDF 1.0–1.7 or 2.0 and a CR/LF boundary before continuing.
Malformed, truncated and unsupported-version prefixes that previously passed
its loose `%PDF-1.`/`%PDF-2.` check now refuse before CUPS launch.

The four focused native Rust tests cover the actual two-page PDF, allowed
version labels, ten malformed sources and three independent fake-spool
refusals. No rejected source file is changed, and production Clippy passes.
This remains a framing check, not a full PDF parser or physical print proof.

## Cross-reference locator refusal before local spool (partial) — 2026-11-04

The explicit `atrament-print` CLI no longer accepts a `%PDF-1.7` header and
terminal `%%EOF` as sufficient evidence of a framed PDF. The same bounded
input snapshot now requires a final `startxref` decimal locator that points
inside the preceding bytes to an `xref` table or a plausible PDF 1.5+
indirect `/Type /XRef` stream header. An ordinary indirect page object,
missing or malformed offset, wrong target and forged EOF-only stub refuse
before contact with CUPS. Native two-page paper and authored-ink PDF output
continues to pass the same check without any byte rewriting.

Five native Rust tests cover the admitted print workflow and added negative
cases. One test checks seven forged/truncated/repointed PDF trailer examples
against an executable fake-spool script, with no launch and unchanged input
bytes. An indirect xref-stream framing case remains eligible, without
claiming complete PDF parsing.

The indirect-stream preflight also now checks an exact PDF `/XRef` name
boundary rather than accepting `/XRefShadow` as an xref stream. An added
negative regression proves that the malformed type refuses both selected
file admission and fake-spool launch, preserving the source bytes. The
PDF grammar itself and earlier object contents are still not validated.

PDF version is now also checked for cross-reference *streams*: a stream
framing with PDF 1.0 or 1.4 in its header refuses, while the same minimal
framing under PDF 1.5 remains eligible. The negative regression first
failed against the old code, then passed with the additional restriction;
both refused cases are checked against fake CUPS invocation and original
file bytes. Legacy table-based PDF framing remains eligible across the
existing supported version range.

The bounded xref-stream header scan now also requires an actual outer
`<< ... >>` dictionary with an exact `/Type /XRef` name entry and a standalone
`stream` token followed by a line break. A red-before/green-after regression
rejects fake type names inside comments, strings, arrays, nested maps, or
outside the dictionary, plus incidental `downstream` and `%stream` text.
The same test admits nested dictionaries, comments, literal strings and CRLF
stream framing in a properly typed indirect stream. All six print tests and
strict all-target paper Clippy pass; fake CUPS remains untouched for refused
inputs and selected bytes remain unchanged.

The traditional `xref` locator now additionally demands at least one complete
fixed-width record in a numbered subsection, followed by a `trailer` and
`<< ... >>` dictionary. A red-before/green-after fixture had accepted a bare
`xref` keyword or partial table as printable; the corrected gate refuses
those forms, bad decimal records, wrong statuses and absent closing trailers
without CUPS side effects. Native two-page and multi-subsection table framing
remain admitted. All seven focused print tests and strict all-target Clippy
pass; this remains a bounded framing check, not a full PDF parser.

The PDF 1.5+ xref-stream path additionally requires a terminal
`endstream` keyword and `endobj` after the stream data, with only whitespace
or comments between the closing delimiters and `startxref`. A typed
xref-stream *header* without its two closing tokens previously passed the
printer preflight; six red-before/green-after fake-CUPS refusals now block
missing, fused or trailing-junk delimiters before spool submission. A
legitimate nested dictionary, CRLF header and terminal comment stay eligible.
This is still lexical framing, not parsing or decoding the stream payload.

The check is a restrictive framing preflight,
not a trustworthy PDF syntax/content validator or printer acceptance proof.
The task stays open for real-device alignment and complete saved overlays.

## Acceptance decision

Open. Provide the actual bounded Rust/backend or browser integration and an
accepted/refused example before marking the executable capability complete.
Theoretical design acceptance alone cannot establish that this feature runs.

## Explicit named local printing (partial) — 2026-11-03

The separate Rust `atrament-print` executable submits an already-exported,
operator-selected PDF to one explicitly selected local CUPS queue. It reads
a single regular file through the existing Linux symlink-refusing file
opener and exact bounded reader; missing/invalid selections, queue names,
PDF framing and 24 MB limit all refuse before invoking `lp`. The submission
has no shell, no implicit printer selection, and sends the exact validated
PDF bytes over standard input rather than reopening a mutable filename.

The standalone `atrament-profiles` notebook exporter already honors
`printed_sheet` on preprinted paper, retains any optional numeric date,
page numbering, border and explicit authored strokes, and omits existing
ruling. Exports still never enqueue printer jobs. The native `atrament-print`
command is the deliberately separate physical-print intent.

**Evidence:** three focused Rust tests use a real two-page, unruled native
PDF and an isolated fake `lp` executable to verify the complete exact byte
stream and printer arguments without contacting a physical printer.
See [local print CLI](../../../technical/local-print-cli.md) for usage and
scope. No real CUPS queue, print alignment or successful physical page
has been verified, and no saved image overlays exist. This executable
task remains **open** pending those criteria.
